  "provenance": {
    "constructionPeriod": "1165–1173.",
    "depictedPhase": "The surviving polygonal keep and three rectangular clasping turrets.",
    "omitted": [
      "Lost curtain and bailey, removed by the 17th–19th centuries",
      "Original dome and spire; replacement lead roof of 1831",
      "Small forebuilding, detailed chambers and local turret heights"
    ],
    "dimensionSource": "docs/plan/building-record.md: central tower 27 m high and 15 m wide. English Heritage, History of Orford Castle (https://www.english-heritage.org.uk/visit/places/orford-castle/history/); Suffolk Heritage Explorer designation DSF15075 (https://heritage.suffolk.gov.uk/Designation/DSF15075), three projecting turrets to NNE, west and southeast.",
    "planClass": "well-published-survey",
    "confidence": "scholarly-reconstruction",
    "note": "Original twelve-sided simplification of the irregular core, with schematic 5 × 5 m radial turrets and a 3 m core wall. Three equal 9 m levels and a straight stair approximate the principal storeys. Main entrance is on the southwest-facing flank of the southeast turret; its approach bearing follows that coarse opening. Flat open caps avoid inventing the lost dome and spire or presenting the 1831 lead roof as medieval."
  }
